// `case _:` parses, so the emitter says the same as the parser does for `case 1:` (KR-164): an integer subject is
// not an enum. It used to say "`match` subject's type could not be resolved — bind it to a typed local first",
// which the typed local `n` already is.
fn int32 main() {
    int32 n = 2;
    int32 r = match (n) { case _: 0; };   // -> REJECT
    return r;
}
